---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const asideLinks = [
  { label: 'Independence', href: '#independence' },
  { label: 'Sources and verification', href: '#sources-and-verification' },
  { label: 'How content is produced', href: '#how-content-is-produced' },
  { label: 'Dates and authorship', href: '#dates-and-authorship' },
  { label: 'Advertising and affiliates', href: '#advertising-and-affiliates' },
  { label: 'Contact', href: '#contact' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  metaTitle="Editorial Policy: Sources, Review and Independence"
  description="How FixProblemNow selects topics, checks official documentation, orders safety steps, dates guides, and handles independence and advertising."
  canonical="/editorial-policy/"
  eyebrow="Standards"
  intro="This policy explains the standards behind every guide: where information comes from, how steps are checked, how risk is handled, and what the site will not claim."
  updated="2026-11-15"
  asideLinks={asideLinks}
  asideNote="A correction changes the guide’s update date. See the corrections policy for how to report one."
>
  <h2 id="independence">Independence</h2>
  <p>
    FixProblemNow is an independent publication. It is not owned by, operated by, or officially
    endorsed by any device manufacturer, software vendor, internet provider, or repair company.
    Manufacturer names and product names are used to describe devices, menus, and official support
    resources.
  </p>
  <p>
    Editorial decisions are not sold. No company can pay for a favourable ranking, a positive
    conclusion, or the removal of a safety warning. If the site ever accepts advertising or
    affiliate links, those arrangements will be disclosed on the page where they appear and on the
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources-and-verification">Sources and verification</h2>
  <p>
    Guides rely primarily on first-party documentation: Microsoft Support, Apple Support, Google
    Support, manufacturer support pages, and documentation for the specific protocol or standard
    involved. Each published guide lists the sources used for its key claims.
  </p>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Prefer official documentation over forum posts, videos, or aggregator sites.</li>
    <li>Do not present an unverified cause as a confirmed diagnosis.</li>
    <li>Do not recommend unofficial firmware, modified system images, or driver bundles.</li>
    <li>Describe menu differences when Windows, macOS, Android, or iOS versions diverge.</li>
    <li>Link to the manufacturer for model-specific recovery, warranty, and hardware procedures.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not claim to have physically tested every device, app version, or network configuration
    mentioned on the site. The standard is documented behaviour plus a clear, safety-reviewed
    procedure. Where hands-on testing has not happened, the guide does not invent a test result.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-content-is-produced">How content is produced</h2>
  <p>
    A guide starts with a symptom that can be described without technical vocabulary. The editor
    identifies the smallest set of plausible causes, checks the relevant official tools, and orders
    the steps from least to most disruptive. Reversible checks come before resets, and resets come
    before erasing data or opening hardware.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drafting and editing may involve software tools, including automated checks for links, spelling,
    and structure. Publication requires a human review of the instructions, safety notes, and
    sources. Automation is not used to generate fictional experiences, device test results, author
    identities, or customer reviews.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every guide must include symptoms, possible causes, preparation, a safety section where needed,
    ordered steps, alternative paths, verification, a stopping point, frequently asked questions,
    related guides, and sources. A page that lacks these elements is not ready to publish.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dates-and-authorship">Dates and authorship</h2>
  <p>
    Publication and update dates come from the guide’s metadata. We do not stagger dates to make the
    library appear more active, and we do not backdate content. When a guide is materially changed,
    its update date changes. A typo fix may be made without a visible date change if the guidance
    itself is unchanged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Guides are published under the FixProblemNow name rather than invented personal bylines. The
    site does not claim professional certifications, medical qualifications, manufacturer
    authorisation, or hands-on experience that it cannot substantiate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="advertising-and-affiliates">Advertising and affiliates</h2>
  <p>
    At the time of publication, the site contains no advertising, sponsored content, or affiliate
    links. If affiliate links are introduced, they will be clearly disclosed before the link, and
    the editorial conclusion will remain independent of commission. We will not recommend a product
    solely because it pays a commission, and we will not hide a paid relationship in a generic
    disclosure page alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Report a factual error, an outdated step, or a missing safety warning through the
    <a href="/contact/?topic=correction">contact form</a>. Include the guide URL, your device model,
    the software version, and the exact text or step that is wrong. The
    <a href="/corrections-policy/">corrections policy</a> explains the review process.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
